1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,880
All right, so on the research blog earlier this week we published an article about a

2
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:17,160
new web threat called HTTP3 connection contamination.

3
00:00:17,160 --> 00:00:24,280
And I was trying to think of some kind of beer analogy to make this make sense and nothing

4
00:00:24,280 --> 00:00:25,280
really stuck.

5
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:31,000
And then I realized actually this attack is so simple it doesn't need an analogy I can

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:35,960
just explain it in the full technical details in one slide in four minutes and it will make

7
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:39,380
perfect sense to everybody.

8
00:00:39,380 --> 00:00:43,520
So we're going to start off with a HTTP history lesson.

9
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:50,980
HTTP is the language that BIRP and also web browsers like Chrome and Firefox speak to

10
00:00:50,980 --> 00:00:53,320
websites.

11
00:00:53,320 --> 00:00:57,880
And every HTTP message is sent over a connection.

12
00:00:57,880 --> 00:01:01,600
You don't really need to worry about what a connection actually is.

13
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:08,080
You only need to know one thing, which is that creating a connection is slow.

14
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:14,160
So the original standardized version of HTTP was called 1.0.

15
00:01:14,160 --> 00:01:20,640
It came out in 96 and each message was sent over its own unique connection.

16
00:01:20,640 --> 00:01:24,520
And everything was nice and secure and wonderful.

17
00:01:24,520 --> 00:01:27,760
But then people were like, well, this is too slow.

18
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:29,960
We want to make this go faster.

19
00:01:29,960 --> 00:01:38,200
And so they thought, well, what if we start sending multiple messages over each connection,

20
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:42,740
provided that all the messages are going to the same website.

21
00:01:42,740 --> 00:01:47,300
And that was formalized in HTTP 1.1 in 96.

22
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:50,420
And it definitely made things faster.

23
00:01:50,420 --> 00:01:54,700
And it also introduced a terrible vulnerability called request smuggling, which has been making

24
00:01:54,700 --> 00:01:57,300
hackers happy ever since.

25
00:01:57,300 --> 00:02:04,860
And well, we've been using HTTP 1.1 for a long time.

26
00:02:04,860 --> 00:02:10,860
A large portion of the web still uses 1.1 and is still vulnerable to request smuggling.

27
00:02:10,860 --> 00:02:20,900
But eventually they figured out how to stop request smuggling and they put it in HTTP 2.

28
00:02:20,900 --> 00:02:23,740
And that solved request smuggling more or less.

29
00:02:23,740 --> 00:02:28,900
But they couldn't resist also trying to make things a little bit faster.

30
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:35,940
And they said, OK, why don't we start using a single connection for multiple different websites,

31
00:02:35,940 --> 00:02:40,340
as long as those websites are closely related to each other?

32
00:02:40,340 --> 00:02:43,060
And yep, sure enough, that made things faster.

33
00:02:43,060 --> 00:02:50,340
But it also introduced a new vulnerability called HP connection contamination.

34
00:02:52,340 --> 00:03:01,380
The reason that this happens is that ever since 97 or earlier, websites have been able to assume

35
00:03:01,380 --> 00:03:06,500
that all the requests sent over a single connection were intended for the same website.

36
00:03:07,620 --> 00:03:10,100
And some of them just started taking that for granted.

37
00:03:10,100 --> 00:03:17,140
And so now the browsers have started suddenly sending messages for different websites down the same

38
00:03:17,700 --> 00:03:23,060
connection. The end result is the server doesn't realize this and it ends up going to the wrong website.

39
00:03:24,660 --> 00:03:29,780
Now, you might be thinking, well, if we just publish this technique and that change happened in 2015,

40
00:03:29,780 --> 00:03:31,380
why is there that big gap?

41
00:03:32,180 --> 00:03:38,100
And the answer is, well, because the websites have to be closely related to each other,

42
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:43,060
this issue doesn't happen very much in practice.

43
00:03:43,060 --> 00:03:47,780
It does happen sometimes. That's how I found it, because someone blew up their website by accident

44
00:03:47,780 --> 00:03:52,020
with this and made an angry forum post. And I was like, hang on, there's a security issue here.

45
00:03:53,620 --> 00:03:55,620
But the story doesn't end there.

46
00:03:56,740 --> 00:04:00,900
Because, well, what's going to happen next? We're going to get HTTP 3, aren't we?

47
00:04:00,900 --> 00:04:07,700
And, well, guess what it says in the specification for our for HTTP 3? It says, you know, we love connection reuse so much.

48
00:04:08,740 --> 00:04:17,300
We're going to relax this requirement so that the websites that we will speak to over one connection no longer need to share an IP.

49
00:04:17,860 --> 00:04:21,140
In other words, they no longer need to be closely related.

50
00:04:21,140 --> 00:04:26,020
And basically, it could happen to any two websites hosted by the same company.

51
00:04:26,820 --> 00:04:28,100
And what does that mean?

52
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:35,540
Well, it means this issue that is currently extremely rare is just going to get worse in the near future.

53
00:04:36,660 --> 00:04:38,580
What's the real life impact of this?

54
00:04:38,580 --> 00:04:48,900
Well, basically, if you try and speak to a secure website owned by a company, someone may have hacked one of their insecure websites,

55
00:04:49,540 --> 00:04:55,140
and you may end up speaking to the one that's been hacked, and bad things are going to happen.

56
00:04:55,780 --> 00:04:59,060
And that is HTTP 3 connection contamination.

57
00:04:59,060 --> 00:05:00,100
Thanks for listening.

